[Event "Italian opening: the quiet Italian"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/vMvQfAPq/p7x52xz5"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Today we learn the Italian: king pawn out, knight out, then the bishop swings across to aim at the weak square beside his king. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { Straight into the middle. Your pawn takes both green squares away from his pieces, and it opens a road for your bishop and queen. [%csl Gd5,Gf5] } (1. d4 { Also excellent. The queen pawn grabs just as much of the middle, and it opens the door to a whole different world of openings. } 1... d5 2. c4 { That is the queens gambit: you offer a wing pawn to drag his centre pawn away from the middle. A fine opening, just not today. }) (1. Nf3 { A good move too: your knight guards two middle squares straight away. It is simply slower than putting your own pawn there first. } 1... d5 2. d4 { That is a queen pawn opening, and perfectly sound. Today, though, you want your bishop out early and glaring at his king. }) 1... e5 { He copies you and plants a pawn in the middle too. Bring a knight out where it attacks that pawn and gets you ready to castle. [%cal Yg1f3] } 2. Nf3 { Knights before bishops. Your knight already attacks his centre pawn, and it is one of the two pieces that must move before you castle. [%cal Gf3e5] } (2. Bc4 { Your bishop is happy on that square, and you play it next move anyway. Knights usually come out before bishops, though. } 2... Nf6 3. d3 { His knight hits your king pawn, so a small pawn step guards it. Nothing is lost, and your own knight is still ready to come out. }) (2. Qh5 { Never send your queen out this early. She is the biggest piece you own, and every little move he makes can chase her about. } 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 g6 { His pawn shoves your queen away, and while she runs he is building a home for his bishop. You have handed him free moves. }) (2. f4 { The kings gambit: you offer the pawn beside your king to rip the middle open. Wild, famous, and a lesson for another day. } 2... exf4 { He takes, so he is a pawn up and the game turns crazy. Great fun, but there is a lot to know before you play like that. }) (2. d4 { The centre game. He simply takes your pawn, and winning it back will cost you time. } 2... exd4 3. Nf3 { Quite playable: you have given a pawn to get your pieces out quickly. In the Italian you never hand a centre pawn over at all. }) 2... Nc6 { He guards his pawn with a knight. Now bring your bishop out to stare at the red square, the softest spot beside his king. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yf1c4] } 3. Bc4 { The Italian! Your bishop points straight at the red square, and only his king is guarding it. That is the whole idea. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gc4f7] } (3. Bb5 { The Spanish, and every bit as strong. Your bishop leans on the knight that guards his centre pawn, but it is a much longer story. } 3... a6 4. Ba4 { Excellent chess, and a huge opening in its own right. Today you want the bishop aiming at the red square straight away. [%csl Rf7] }) (3. d4 { The Scotch: you rip the middle open at once instead of building slowly. Also good, and another opening for another day. } 3... exd4 4. Nxd4 { Fine for you, and your knight stands proudly in the middle. Today, though, your bishop goes hunting beside his king instead. }) 3... Bc5 { He copies you, and his bishop now stares at the pawn beside your king. Push this little pawn: it gets ready to help a big centre. [%cal Yc2c3] } 4. c3 { A quiet move with a big idea. It gets a second pawn ready to step onto the green square, so you own the whole middle. [%csl Gd4] } (4. Bxf7+ { Greedy. Check first and think later, and your bishop is gone for a single pawn with nothing at all to show for it. } 4... Kxf7 { His king takes the bishop and stands there perfectly safely. You are a piece down for one pawn. }) (4. Ng5 { Too soon. Nothing at all is guarding your knight out there, and his queen has a clear road to it. } 4... Qxg5 { His queen strolls over and eats it for free. Jump in only when the knight is guarded, or when the red square is truly falling apart. [%csl Rf7] }) (4. d3 { Completely fine, and you play this pawn step anyway. It just reaches the same set up in a different order. } 4... Nf6 5. c3 { Both pawn moves are made and you land in the same place. The order hardly matters, so play whichever one you remember. }) (4. b4 { The Evans gambit: you throw a wing pawn at his bishop to win time for a big centre. Wild fun, and a lesson for another day. } 4... Bxb4 5. c3 { Sharp stuff, and your pawn shoves his bishop about while you build. Today you stay calm and keep every pawn you own. }) (4. O-O { Safe and good. Castling early is almost never wrong, but your king pawn needs a guard once his knight comes out. } 4... Nf6 5. d3 { The small pawn step guards your king pawn, and your king is already safe. You are one little pawn push away from the set-up you are learning. }) 4... Nf6 { His knight attacks your king pawn. Guard it with a small pawn step: the big push in the middle can wait. [%cal Yd2d3] } 5. d3 { That little step guards your king pawn and opens a road for your last bishop. The push to the green square simply waits. [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd3e4] } (5. O-O { Castling is good, but right now his knight can snap off your king pawn, because nothing at all is guarding it. } 5... Nxe4 { He grabs it. You can chase the knight and win the pawn back, but he is the comfortable one. That small step stops all of this. }) (5. d4 { The big centre, right now. A real line and far sharper than ours, with the pawns crashing into each other at once. } 5... exd4 6. e5 { Exciting, and just as good, but an awful lot to remember. The quiet pawn step keeps things simple. }) (5. Ng5 { Too early. His king simply castles, and your knight is left out there on its own. } 5... O-O { His rook now guards the pawn beside his king as well, and your knight has nothing to bite on. }) 5... d6 { His small pawn step guards his centre pawn too. Every piece on your king side is out, so now make your king safe. [%cal Ye1g1] } 6. O-O { Your king is tucked in the corner and your rook joins the game. Castle early: it is almost always the right idea. [%csl Gg1] } (6. Bb3 { Also fine. You tuck the bishop away before he ever gets the chance to chase it with a pawn. } 6... O-O 7. O-O { You castle one move later, with your bishop already tucked back where it is harder to chase. }) (6. Nbd2 { The sleepy knight comes out first, and that is just as good: nothing here is urgent, so your king can wait a move. } 6... O-O 7. O-O { Both kings are tucked away, and this is the very same position you get by castling first. The order changed, nothing else did. }) (6. Bg5 { Your bishop pins his knight against his queen. A fine move, but he shoves it about with his pawns and gains time doing it. } 6... h6 7. Bh4 g5 { His pawns chase your bishop and grab space, though they leave his king rather airy. We keep things simpler than that. }) (6. h3 { A useful little move, and quite playable. It takes away the green square, where his bishop and knight both like to jump. [%csl Gg4] } 6... O-O 7. O-O { You castle anyway one move later, and that small pawn move stays useful for the rest of the game. }) (6. Ng5 { Your knight and bishop both hit the pawn beside his king, but castling saves it. } 6... O-O 7. Nf3 { Two moves for nothing: your knight is back where it started, while he has tucked his king safely away. }) 6... O-O { Both kings are home. Your queen side knight has not moved yet, so bring it out. [%cal Yb1d2] } 7. Nbd2 { Your last knight joins in. Both kings are safe, a small pawn guards your king pawn, and the Italian is built. } (7. Bb3 { Just as good. Parking the bishop there now means his queen pawn can never come along and hit it. } 7... a6 8. Nbd2 { Your knight comes out one move later and nothing has gone wrong. His little pawn move gives his bishop a hiding place. }) (7. h3 { Also fine, and very normal. That step keeps his bishop and knight off the green square. [%csl Gg4] } 7... a6 8. Nbd2 { The sleepy knight follows one move later, and you have a useful little pawn move in the bank. }) (7. Re1 { Good too. Your rook slides in behind the king pawn, where the position usually breaks open later on. } 7... a6 8. Nbd2 { The knight still comes out, and now your rook is doing something useful as well. A fine way to play it. }) (7. Ng5 { The knight jumps forward, but his king and rook both guard the pawn beside his king. } 7... h6 8. Nf3 { His pawn shoos the knight straight home. You have thrown two moves away and he made a useful one. }) 1-0


[Event "Italian opening: the fried liver"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/vMvQfAPq/1VLehfWQ"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 3 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_3_3"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Today you learn a famous Italian attack called the fried liver. Your knight and bishop are out, and it is his move: watch his other knight. }
3... Nf6 { His knight jumps out at your king pawn. Do not defend it: send your knight forward and make trouble instead. [%cal Yf3g5] } 4. Ng5 { Two of your pieces now stare at the red square, and only his king guards it. Your knight would love to jump in and fork his queen and rook. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7] } (4. d3 { Solid, safe and sleepy. A perfectly good move, but there is something far sharper sitting in this position right now. } 4... Bc5 5. c3 { A fine quiet game that you can play happily. It is simply not the sharp attack that was on offer a move ago. }) (4. O-O { Castling is good, but he grabs your king pawn straight away, because nothing at all is guarding it. } 4... Nxe4 { He is a pawn up for now, and you can win it back. The knight jump was the sharp moment, and that moment has gone. [%cal Gf3g5] }) (4. Bxf7+ { Never just hand the bishop over like this. A check is not a plan, and the bishop does not come back. } 4... Kxf7 { A bishop for one pawn, and no attack at all: he is simply a piece up, and his king is in no danger. }) (4. d4 { A real move, and a whole different game: you smash the middle open instead of going after his king. } 4... exd4 5. e5 { Perfectly playable, and a big wild fight. Today, though, you want the red square, because that is where he is weakest. [%csl Rf7] }) 4... d5 { His best answer. Grabbing your king pawn instead would have lost to a check from your bishop. Take with your pawn. [%cal Ye4d5] } 5. exd5 { Taking with the pawn is the point: your bishop stays on the board, and your pawn now hits his knight. [%cal Gd5c6] } (5. Bxd5 { The wrong capture. Look at your own knight out there, deep in his half, with nothing at all guarding it. } 5... Nxd5 6. exd5 Qxg5 7. dxc6 Qxg2 8. Rf1 Bh3 { You won his knight back, but his queen and bishop now threaten to take your rook with mate. You are losing. [%csl Rh3][%cal Rg2f1] }) (5. Nxf7 { Too early. That pawn he shoved into the middle attacks your bishop, so it has to come off the board first. } 5... Kxf7 6. exd5 { You gave a knight for two pawns, his king stands safely, and there is nothing left of the attack. }) (5. d3 { You have forgotten the pawn he just shoved into the middle: it is attacking your bishop right now. [%csl Rd5] } 5... dxc4 { That pawn simply takes your bishop, and a single pawn is all you can win back for it. }) 5... Nxd5 { He takes back with the knight, and that is the mistake: his other knight should have gone after your bishop. Now jump in. [%cal Yg5f7] } 6. Nxf7 { The fried liver: you hand over a knight to drag his king out. He must take, because it is forking his queen and his rook. [%cal Gf7d8,Gf7h8] } (6. Bxd5 { You win his knight and he takes your bishop straight back. Material stays level, but his pieces will run the whole board. } 6... Qxd5 7. O-O Bf5 { His queen sits proudly in the middle, his bishop comes out, and your knight is stranded. Nobody is a piece up, but he is clearly better. }) (6. O-O { Castling is fine in most positions, but here you have forgotten your own knight, sitting out there unguarded. } 6... Qxg5 7. Bxd5 { Your bishop takes his knight back, so material is level, but his queen is out and the fried liver has gone. }) (6. d4 { Just as strong: your pawn hits his centre, and the knight jump can still come later. } 6... Nxd4 7. c3 { Your pawn hits his knight in the middle, and you stand just as well as after the knight jump. }) 6... Kxf7 { He takes, because leaving your knight there costs him his queen or his rook. Now check at once, before he can catch his breath. [%cal Yd1f3] } 7. Qf3+ { Check, and your queen lines up on his knight in the middle as well. He has to answer the check first. [%cal Gf3d5] } (7. Qh5+ { A check, but from the wrong square: it pushes his king back towards safety instead of keeping it out in the open. } 7... Kg8 { His king steps home, your attack is over, and you gave a knight for one pawn to get here. }) (7. Bxd5+ { The pinned knight looked tasty, but his queen simply takes your bishop back, and the whole attack comes off with it. } 7... Qxd5 { You are a whole piece down for one pawn with no attack left at all. This was the losing move, not the winning one. }) (7. Nc3 { A fine developing move in most positions. Here it hands him a free move, and a free move is all he needs. } 7... Be6 { His bishop comes out and guards his knight, and the attack is gone. The check had to come first. }) (7. O-O { Far too slow. His king is out in the open for one move only, and you have let that move slip by. } 7... Be6 { He tidies his pieces up, his king is safe again, and you are simply a piece down for a pawn. }) 7... Ke6 { His king must walk out into the middle to defend the knight. Now bring your last knight into the attack. [%cal Yb1c3] } 8. Nc3 { Your knight piles onto the pinned knight, and his king is stuck out in the open with nowhere safe to sit. [%tail][%csl Re6][%cal Gc3d5] } (8. Bxd5+ { Taking looks tempting, but his queen takes your bishop back, the queens come off, and the attack is gone. } 8... Qxd5 9. Qxd5+ Kxd5 { His king stands safely in the middle with the queens gone, and you are a piece down for one pawn. }) (8. d4 { Natural, and it loses the thread: his knight jumps into the middle and hits your queen at once. } 8... Nxd4 { Your queen has to move, the attack dies with her, and he keeps the whole piece he is up. }) (8. O-O { Castling is much too slow here. His king is only out in the open for a moment, and you let the moment pass. } 8... b5 { His pawn attacks the bishop that holds the pin, and you are still a whole piece down for a pawn. }) 8... Nb4 9. O-O c6 10. d4 Qf6 11. Qe2 Ke7 12. dxe5 { Your pawn takes a second pawn and hits his queen. A knight for two pawns, and his king can never castle: that is the fried liver. [%csl Re7][%cal Ge5f6] } 1-0


[Event "Italian opening: he plays too slowly"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/vMvQfAPq/9JZcZY19"]
[FEN "r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P3/2P2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P3/2P2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_0_4"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ Your small pawn step in the Italian has prepared a big centre. It is his move: if he plays slowly, make him pay. }
4... d6 { He answers with a modest little pawn move instead of bringing his knight out. He is playing slowly: how do you punish that? } 5. d4 { Exactly! Two pawns on the green squares, and his bishop is attacked as well. His knight should have come out to hit your king pawn. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (5. d3 { Fine, but far too polite. He played slowly, so this was the moment to grab the whole middle, not nudge a pawn one step. } 5... Nf6 6. O-O { A level, comfortable game for both of you. The big push was the punishment, and it has quietly slipped away. }) (5. O-O { Safe and good, but it misses the moment. Castling can wait a move; the chance to punish his slow pawn move cannot. } 5... Nf6 6. d4 { You play the big push anyway, but now he has a piece out and it annoys him far less than it should have. }) (5. Ng5 { Nothing out there guards your knight, and his queen has a clear road all the way to it. } 5... Qxg5 { His queen takes it for free. Count the guards before you send a piece deep into his half of the board. }) (5. Bxf7+ { A bishop for one pawn is a bad trade, and the check achieves nothing at all here. } 5... Kxf7 { His king is safe enough where it stands, and you are a bishop down for one pawn with no attack to show. }) (5. Qb3 { Your queen and bishop both aim at the weak pawn beside his king, and your queen hits a wing pawn too. } 5... Qe7 { His queen guards the pawn beside his king, and his bishop already guards the wing pawn. Your big chance in the middle has slipped by. }) 5... exd4 { He takes in the middle. Three of your men can take that pawn back: which one leaves your centre strongest? } 6. cxd4 { The pawn takes back, and two pawns now stand side by side on the green squares. That pair is gold in the Italian. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (6. Nxd4 { The knight takes, and your lovely pawn pair vanishes before it was ever built. } 6... Nf6 7. O-O { About level, and his pieces are the happier ones. Building that pawn pair was the whole point of your small pawn step. }) (6. e5 { Too fast. That pawn is attacked twice and guarded only once, so he simply wins it. } 6... Nxe5 7. Nxe5 dxe5 { He takes twice, and you are two pawns down: the one he ate and the one you never took back. }) (6. O-O { Castling is never silly, but his pawn was hanging in the middle, and it is still hanging now. } 6... dxc3 7. Nxc3 { Your knight takes back, and you are a pawn down for quicker pieces. The tidy pawn pair has gone for good. }) (6. Qxd4 { Careful: his bishop is aiming at that square, and your queen is worth far more than a pawn. [%cal Rc5d4] } 6... Nxd4 7. cxd4 { His knight took your queen, and your pawn only got the knight back. A queen for a knight is a terrible trade. }) 6... Bb6 { His bishop steps back out of the way, and your pawn pair stands in the middle. What next? } 7. O-O { Your king is safe, you own a big centre, and most of his pieces are still sitting at home. The Italian has done its job. } (7. h3 { Also fine, and useful later: that little step keeps his bishop and knight off the green square. [%csl Gg4] } 7... Nf6 8. O-O { You castle anyway one move later, and the pawn step will save you trouble down the line. }) (7. Bb5 { A good square too: from there your bishop leans on the knight instead. Castling was the simpler job. } 7... Bd7 8. Nc3 { Sound enough, though your king is still standing in the middle. Tuck it away first and then finish developing. }) (7. d5 { Pushing past hands his knight a lovely square right in front of your pawn, and it locks the centre you just won. } 7... Ne5 8. Nxe5 dxe5 { Still fine for you, but the pawns are jammed in the middle and the open game has gone. }) (7. Nc3 { Just as good: your sleepy knight comes out and guards your king pawn. } 7... Nf6 8. O-O { You castle one move later, with a knight already out. Nothing is lost. }) (7. Be3 { Also good: your bishop comes out and guards your centre pawn. } 7... Nf6 8. Nc3 { Your knight guards your king pawn from his knight, and your king can still castle. Just as good. }) 1-0


[Event "Italian opening: the plan after castling"]
[Result "1-0"]
