[FEN "r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2np1n2/2b1p3/2B1P3/2PP1N2/PP1N1PPP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 3 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2np1n2/2b1p3/2B1P3/2PP1N2/PP1N1PPP/R1BQ1RK1_b_-_-_3_7"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Both kings are tucked away, so the Italian plan begins: a rook behind the centre pawn, then the sleepy knight goes travelling. }
7... h6 { He makes a little escape square for his king, and now your knight cannot jump forward on that side. Bring a rook into the game instead. [%cal Yf1e1] } 8. Re1 { Your rook lines up behind your king pawn, and the green square is where this position usually breaks open. [%csl Ge4] } (8. d4 { Too early. The break in the middle needs every piece ready first, and yours are not ready yet. } 8... exd4 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 10. cxd4 Bxd4 { His bishop takes your centre pawn, and nothing of yours can take it back. He is a pawn up. }) (8. h3 { Useful too: that little pawn step keeps his knight and bishop off the green square. [%csl Gg4] } 8... a6 9. Re1 { The rook comes anyway one move later, and the little pawn step stays useful for the whole game. }) (8. b4 { Space on the other wing, and quite playable: the pawn pushes at his bishop and takes squares from his knight. } 8... Bb6 9. a4 { A completely different plan, rolling your pawns forward on the queen side. Today your knight goes after his king instead. }) (8. Bb3 { Just as good: your bishop steps back before any pawn can chase it. } 8... a6 9. Re1 { Your rook comes out one move later, with your bishop already tucked back. Just as good. }) 8... a6 { A quiet little move that gives his bishop a hiding place. Nothing is urgent, so your sleepy knight can set off on its trip. [%cal Yd2f1] } 9. Nf1 { The sleepy knight starts its trip round towards his king. One more hop and it joins the attack. [%cal Gd2f1,Gf1g3] } (9. d4 { Too soon. The break in the middle needs your pieces ready, and your knight has not set off yet. } 9... exd4 10. cxd4 Nxd4 11. Nxd4 Bxd4 { He is a clean pawn up, with his bishop sitting in the middle of the board. }) (9. h3 { Also useful, and it costs nothing: your knight can set off on its journey next move instead. } 9... Be6 { Fine as well. His bishop comes out, and your knight still wants that trip round towards his king. [%cal Gd2f1,Gf1g3] }) (9. Bb3 { Also good, and very popular: the bishop steps back to a safer square first. } 9... Be6 10. Nf1 { Your knight sets off one move later. His bishop offers a swap, and that is fine for you. }) (9. a4 { Also good: that pawn takes space on the wing and stops his pawns pushing there. } 9... Ba7 10. Nf1 { Your knight sets off one move later, and the wing pawn stays useful. }) 9... Be6 { His last bishop comes out and offers a swap of bishops. Let him: finish your knight's trip first. [%cal Yf1g3] } 10. Ng3 { Your knight lands on the king side and eyes both green squares. That was the whole trip. [%csl Gf5,Gh5] } (10. Bxe6 { Trading first is fine too, and perhaps even better: his pawn has to take back, and his pawns end up doubled. } 10... fxe6 11. Ng3 { The knight goes travelling anyway, and his two stacked pawns sit one behind the other in the middle. }) (10. d4 { The break, one move too soon: his bishop takes yours, and it is your centre that falls apart, not his. } 10... Bxc4 11. dxc5 dxc5 { Your best piece has been traded off, you are a pawn down, and his pieces are the happy ones. }) (10. Bb3 { Just as good: the bishop steps back, and if he swaps, your queen takes back. } 10... Bxb3 11. Qxb3 { Your queen takes back and looks at the pawn beside his king. The knight's trip can wait a move. }) 10... Bxc4 { His bishop swaps itself for yours, just as it offered to. Take back with the pawn. } 11. dxc4 { Your pawn takes back, and your queen now faces his queen pawn down the column. Your knight eyes both green squares: the Italian plan is complete. [%csl Gf5,Gh5][%cal Gd1d6] } 1-0


[Event "Italian opening: win the knight back"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/vMvQfAPq/y0blSOG6"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r1bq1b1r/ppp2kpp/2n5/3np3/2B5/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R b KQ - 1 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1b1r/ppp2kpp/2n5/3np3/2B5/5Q2/PPPP1PPP/RNB1K2R_b_KQ_-_1_7"]

{ The fried liver: your knight is gone, and your queen has just checked his king. Watch where his king goes. }
7... Ke8 { His king runs back home instead of stepping forward, and that is a mistake. Can you punish it? } 8. Bxd5 { Your bishop takes his knight: once his king ran home, only his queen was guarding it. You are a pawn up. [%cal Gc4d5] } (8. Qxd5 { Your queen takes the knight too, and threatens mate on the red square. [%csl Rf7] } 8... Qf6 { His queen steps out to guard the square beside his king. Still slightly better for you, but taking with the bishop was much stronger. }) (8. Nc3 { Piling on worked when his king stood behind his knight. Here his king has stepped away, so his knight is free to move. } 8... Nf4 { His knight simply steps away, and you are a knight down for a pawn. }) (8. O-O { Castling is too slow: his knight is attacked twice and guarded once, and it will not wait for you. } 8... Nf6 { His knight steps back to safety, and you are a knight down for one pawn. }) 8... Nd4 { His knight attacks your queen, but that is a mistake. What now? } 9. Qf7# { Checkmate! Your bishop guards your queen, and his king has nowhere to go. His queen should have come out to guard the green square. [%csl Gf7][%cal Gd5f7] } (9. Qh5+ { A check, and still good for you, but there was a checkmate on the board. } 9... g6 { He blocks the check, and the mate has gone. You are better, but the game goes on. }) (9. Qd1 { Your queen runs home, and your bishop is left with nothing guarding it. } 9... Qxd5 { His queen takes your bishop, and now you are a piece down. }) (9. Qe4 { Your queen steps into the middle, but his bishop can hit her there. } 9... Bf5 { His bishop chases your queen, his pieces come alive, and now he is clearly better. }) 1-0
